1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,128
Good afternoon.

2
00:00:01,448 --> 00:00:12,944
Today we are presenting the Library Usage Monitoring and Statistics System — a web application built to record, monitor, and analyse how our college library is actually used.

3
00:00:13,264 --> 00:00:25,024
Over the next few minutes we will walk through the problem that started this project, the objectives we set, the technology behind the system, and how both students and library staff use it day to day.

4
00:00:25,974 --> 00:00:30,006
Traditionally, library attendance is recorded by hand.

5
00:00:30,326 --> 00:00:36,590
A student signs a paper logbook on the way in, and staff copy those entries later if a report is needed.

6
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:40,054
That approach works, but it has real limits.

7
00:00:40,374 --> 00:00:52,590
Handwriting can be difficult to read, entries can be skipped when the desk is busy, and nobody can answer a simple question like "how many students are in the library right now" without walking around and counting.

8
00:00:52,910 --> 00:00:57,398
Totals for a whole semester mean adding up pages by hand.

9
00:00:57,718 --> 00:01:06,430
Our project replaces that logbook with a centralised web system, so that every entry and exit is recorded the moment it happens.

10
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:10,260
We set three objectives for this system.

11
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:20,080
The first is to develop the web application itself — a single place where students record their visits and where staff manage the library.

12
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:25,560
Because it runs in a browser, students use it on their own phones with nothing to install.

13
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:29,764
The second objective is systematic data management.

14
00:01:30,084 --> 00:01:39,588
Every member record and every visit is stored in a database rather than on paper, so information can be searched, verified, and reviewed later.

15
00:01:39,908 --> 00:01:47,276
Staff can filter records by department, by academic year, or by a range of dates, and get an answer immediately.

16
00:01:47,976 --> 00:01:50,736
The third objective covers administration.

17
00:01:51,056 --> 00:02:04,928
The system gives library staff a dashboard showing current occupancy and usage statistics, and it generates reports from the stored data — which can be printed as PDF, or downloaded as CSV and Excel files.

18
00:02:05,878 --> 00:02:09,742
Let me explain how the system is built, layer by layer.

19
00:02:10,442 --> 00:02:18,506
What the user sees is built with HTML5 and Tailwind CSS, with plain JavaScript handling the interaction.

20
00:02:18,826 --> 00:02:26,578
The layout is responsive, so the same pages work on a phone at the library entrance and on a desktop at the staff counter.

21
00:02:27,278 --> 00:02:40,838
Behind that, the application runs on PHP 8 on an Apache server, using a front-controller pattern — every request enters through a single file that decides which page or which API endpoint should handle it.

22
00:02:41,158 --> 00:02:47,566
This is also the layer that checks whether a user is logged in and whether they are allowed to see what they are asking for.

23
00:02:48,266 --> 00:02:53,834
The data sits in MySQL, in a database named `library_checkin`.

24
00:02:54,154 --> 00:03:06,010
All access goes through PHP Data Objects with prepared statements, which keeps the values a user types separate from the SQL itself — a standard protection against SQL injection.

25
00:03:06,710 --> 00:03:15,062
Finally, printable reports are produced with the mPDF library, together with PHP's GD extension for image handling.

26
00:03:16,012 --> 00:03:18,532
Now let us follow a student through the system.

27
00:03:19,232 --> 00:03:25,232
A student opens the library portal in any browser — usually on their phone as they walk in.

28
00:03:25,552 --> 00:03:28,912
They sign in with their student ID and password.

29
00:03:29,232 --> 00:03:37,056
If it is their first visit, they can register their profile in the same place: name, department, level, and year.

30
00:03:37,756 --> 00:03:42,436
Once signed in, the main screen shows a single large check-in button.

31
00:03:42,756 --> 00:03:49,068
The student presses it, chooses roughly how long they plan to stay, and confirms.

32
00:03:49,388 --> 00:03:53,108
The system records the exact time and stores the visit.

33
00:03:53,428 --> 00:04:04,492
If the same student presses check-in again while already checked in, the system will not create a duplicate entry — it knows they are already inside, and offers check-out instead.

34
00:04:05,192 --> 00:04:10,304
When they leave, they press the same button to check out, and the visit is closed.

35
00:04:11,254 --> 00:04:18,358
Those steps are simple on the surface, but several features work together behind them to keep the records reliable.

36
00:04:18,678 --> 00:04:34,542
The system manages how long each visit lasts, separates what students may do from what staff may do, keeps a live view of who is currently inside, prevents duplicate or unfinished entries, and preserves the full history of every visit.

37
00:04:34,862 --> 00:04:39,590
The next few sections look at the most important of these in more detail.

38
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:46,180
When a student checks in, they choose a planned duration — for example, two hours.

39
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:54,156
The system will not accept a time that runs past the library's closing hour, so a planned visit always ends within opening times.

40
00:04:54,856 --> 00:05:01,672
If they need longer, they can extend the session by thirty or sixty minutes, again within opening hours.

41
00:05:02,372 --> 00:05:06,476
The most useful part is what happens when someone forgets.

42
00:05:06,796 --> 00:05:13,948
In a paper logbook, a student who leaves without signing out simply leaves an open, unfinished line.

43
00:05:14,268 --> 00:05:18,828
Here, the system closes the visit automatically at closing time.

44
00:05:19,148 --> 00:05:26,924
That means the history contains complete visits with a start and an end, and the average duration statistics stay meaningful.

45
00:05:27,874 --> 00:05:34,330
For library staff, the change is that the handwritten logbook is replaced by database records.

46
00:05:34,650 --> 00:05:43,626
Staff can see current occupancy at any moment, open any student's attendance history, and compare usage between departments.

47
00:05:44,326 --> 00:05:55,366
The system also records failed login attempts in a dedicated table, which supports rate-limiting — repeated wrong passwords are slowed down rather than allowed to continue indefinitely.

48
00:05:55,686 --> 00:06:02,478
Changes to the database structure are tracked in a migrations table, so the schema can be updated in a controlled way.

49
00:06:03,178 --> 00:06:16,882
The figure shown here, one hundred percent digital traceability, describes the design goal of this project: that every recorded visit exists as a digital record that can be traced, rather than as a line of handwriting.

50
00:06:17,832 --> 00:06:29,256
Because every visit is stored with a timestamp, the dashboard can group them and show when the library is busiest across the day — morning, midday, afternoon, and evening.

51
00:06:29,956 --> 00:06:38,068
The values on this slide are illustrative, shown to demonstrate how the chart presents a busy midday period against a quieter evening.

52
00:06:38,388 --> 00:06:50,172
The point is the capability: once real attendance data accumulates, staff can see genuine patterns and use them when planning seating or deciding when more staff are needed at the desk.

53
00:06:51,122 --> 00:06:53,690
Not everyone sees the same system.

54
00:06:54,010 --> 00:06:59,290
Students can check in and out, and view their own attendance history — nothing more.

55
00:06:59,610 --> 00:07:05,274
They cannot open the dashboard, browse other students' records, or export reports.

56
00:07:05,974 --> 00:07:17,878
Library staff have full access: the dashboard and analytics, member management, the complete attendance history, and report export in PDF, CSV, and Excel.

57
00:07:18,198 --> 00:07:26,670
The login page is the same for both — the system reads the role attached to the account and decides what that person is allowed to reach.

58
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:30,668
We built the system in four phases.

59
00:07:31,368 --> 00:07:42,648
Phase one was the database — designing the tables for students, users, and check-in logs, and setting up migrations so the structure could change safely later.

60
00:07:43,348 --> 00:07:52,588
Phase two was the core web application: the PHP backend, session-based authentication, the check-in flow, and the Tailwind interface.

61
00:07:53,288 --> 00:08:01,280
Phase three added the administrative side — the dashboard, mPDF report export, and rate-limiting on login.

62
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:04,668
Phase four was testing and deployment.

63
00:08:04,988 --> 00:08:16,532
We tested locally with XAMPP, and deployment to the live server runs through GitHub Actions, so a release is a controlled, repeatable step rather than copying files by hand.

64
00:08:17,482 --> 00:08:25,858
In conclusion, the Library Usage Monitoring and Statistics System replaces a paper logbook with a working web application.

65
00:08:26,178 --> 00:08:40,002
Students check in and out from their own phones, every visit is stored in a central database, staff can see who is in the library right now, and the system turns that stored data into statistics and printable reports.

66
00:08:40,702 --> 00:08:42,238
Thank you for your attention.

67
00:08:42,558 --> 00:08:44,814
We are now ready to answer your questions.

